<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nnamdi Azikiwe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nnamdi Azikiwe, known as Zik, was a leading Nigerian nationalist and journalist and the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Through his newspapers, starting with the West African Pilot in 1937, and the National Council of Nigeria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a>, he campaigned for independence. He was premier of the Eastern Region, governor-general from 1960 and president of the republic from 1963 until the military coup of 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nnamdi-azikiwe.jpg" alt="Nnamdi Azikiwe" width="330" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kambai Akau · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nnamdi_Azikiwe_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nnamdi Azikiwe was born on 16 November 1904 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>. His first name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> and means my father is alive. His father, a clerk in the British administration, came from Onitsha, and his mother was descended from a royal family there. He grew up speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, learned Igbo in Onitsha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he was known as Ben Azikiwe, he studied at Storer College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>, the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Howard University, and he competed in athletics, boxing and other sports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1934 and worked as a journalist in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, advocating Nigerian and African nationalism. After he backed the general strike of June 1945, publication of his paper, the West African Pilot, was suspended for a month. He warned of an assassination plot and went into hiding, and his circulation rose, though some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> politicians doubted the story. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-macaulay">Herbert Macaulay</a> died, Azikiwe took over the NCNC, the nationalist party, and led its delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> against Governor Arthur Richards's constitution. He became premier of the Eastern Region in 1954, and his government founded the African Continental Bank, the first indigenous Nigerian bank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Parliament passed the Nigerian Independence Act on 29 July 1960, and Azikiwe served as governor-general until 1963. When the Federal Republic was established on 1 October 1963, the constitution deemed him elected president, a ceremonial head of state beside Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-tafawa-balewa">Abubakar Tafawa Balewa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> dispute and the December 1964 election, marked by boycotts, rigging and violence, broke the coalition of the NPC and the NCNC, and for several days Azikiwe refused to call Balewa to form a government.</p><p>In 1966 a military coup on 29 July brought Yakubu Gowon to power. Azikiwe died on 11 May 1996. He is widely regarded as the father of Nigerian nationalism, and his ideas, called Zikism, rest on five canons: spiritual balance, social regeneration, economic determinism, mental emancipation and political resurgence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nnamdi Azikiwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalist politics grew around Herbert Macaulay and <b>Nnamdi Azikiwe</b>, and the constitutions of 1946, 1951 and 1954 created a federation of Northern, Western and Eastern regions.</blockquote><a href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nnamdi Azikiwe</b> replaced the governor-general in November 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/nigeria">Nigeria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.8,6.15&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Zungeru on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4720 1798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nnamdi Azikiwe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 2068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 2068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.8,6.15&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
